@0    = ~Do you ever miss Quayle, Aerie?~
@1    = ~Yes, often. After all, he was the one who taught me all I knew at first, about magic, adventuring, and the outside world. If it wasn't for that, I wouldn't be here with <CHARNAME> today.~
@2    = ~Do you believe it's all because of him that you're now a strong and successful adventurer?~
@3    = ~Not entirely, but... I think his guidance shaped me, Will. Not in the way you control spirits and their energy, but it's as though he took my broken spirit and... repaired it, filled in what was missing. Thanks to him, even when I lost hope in everything, I still had strength thanks to him.~
@4    = ~But you're the one who uses your strength to restore yourself. No one does it for you.~
@5    = ~I wouldn't have been able to if not for Quayle, or <CHARNAME>, or anyone else that's been so kind to me. I'll never forget that truth.~
@6    = ~I can still feel a great amount of anger in you, Anomen. You are different - far more dangerous - than the man you once were.~
@7    = ~Bah! What else can one feel, I wonder, when all that he can taste is the bitterness of blood and ashes? This is reality, shaman. One day, should you see the real colors of this world, perhaps you'll be shaken from your sleep.~
@8    = ~And you believe that you see more clearly as you are now?~
@9    = ~More than ever, ghost. Once, I was a blind and foolish child, easily influenced, but those fantasies are behind me now. I see everything as it truly is, past what others claim it to be.~
@10   = ~It's sad to see how much your bitterness has consumed you, Anomen. I feel sorry for you.~
@11   = ~What do you think the people of Athkatla are like today, Anomen, after all that has happened to them?~
@12   = ~There will be peace for some time, I hope, even if it will not last. Bodhi and her coven were a blight that I am glad to see removed. Now if only the same could be done for the Shadow Thieves, the City of Coin may truly be the shining city it claims to be.~
@13   = ~But weren't they a force holding the vampire coven back from total control over the city? Things would have been much worse without the Shadow Thieves.~
@14   = ~One good deed hardly absolves a man from a lifetime of sin, Will, that is one thing you should understand. They have always been thieves and murderers, scoundrels and cheats, and I hardly expect that to change.~
@15   = ~I doubt that even if you eliminate the current guild, things will become right. More will always come. Thieves are ever present in every city, no?~
@16   = ~True, but that knowledge will not deter me from doing what I can.~
@17   = ~The Tree of Life must have made a huge impression on you, Cernd. Care to share your thoughts?~
@18   = ~I believe we were all impressed in our own way by the sight, Will. Perhaps not all appreciate it as well as I do, but the Tree stood as a symbol of the power of balance and nature's gifts.~
@19   = ~I still remember facing those parasites preying on the Tree. We've faced many cruel creatures, but those things feeding on it for their own cruel purpose... if I were still alive, my heart would have bled at the sight.~
@20   = ~But the Tree survived thanks to our efforts. Do you recall the seeds, Will, that grew into the paths of hope that led towards our goal? Irenicus would never have won that day.~
@21   = ~Maybe, but I can't deny he came quite close to it.~
@22   = ~So, what do you see in your future, Red Wizard? Will you become the next Irenicus, causing destruction in your path towards greater power?~
@23   = ~*snort* I have greater ambitions than trying to attain divinity through draining the sap of some elven tree, ghost. I will make my name one to be feared through my own means, without dependency on lesser powers.~
@24   = ~Do you even understand what we fought for? The severity of what Irenicus tried to do? What we saved wasn't a simple tree, Edwin, it was the Tree of Life, the source of life for the elves.~
@25   = ~Bah. The elves may worship and depend on it, but to myself, it is little more than a tree. Greater powers take upon more distinguished forms, but the fool attempted to sap powers he had no true understanding of. (No doubt he had become fully delusional as a result of carrying <CHARNAME>''s soul...)~
@26   = ~You're not just ignorant, Edwin, but you are also a fool. I only hope the only one to come to harm from those failings will be you.~
@27   = ~I warn you, gibbon, one more disparaging remark and I will grant you a body. Oh yes, a body of stone, to be victimized by the droppings of countless avian, no doubt! Begone!~
@28   = ~I'm qute surprised that you asked, Haer'Dalis. Yes, I loved Higam. I never loved another the same way after his death.~
@29   = ~So entropy severed the bond between you and that man who captured your heart. Tragic, but beautiful. Although I should say... 'tis good to see you have preserved your feelings in your current state before they were ruined by... unwelcome disruptions.~
@30   = ~Somehow I doubt any "unwelcome disruptions" would have happened regardless. I don't consider the bond between myself and my beloved broken either. I believe it's still there, but invisible.~
@31   = ~You do seem rather confident in that. But perhaps that is good. Such things are as we believe them to be, and they are stronger that way.~
@32   = ~I agree. I might have said it before, but... I didn't live without pleasure after losing my love. I slept with many others to find comfort, but I never felt anything half as close. My soul and mind will always belong to him.~
@33   = ~A fine tragedy that the tale of your life would make, my friend. I can see the tears that would no doubt be shed even now.~
@34   = ~You also knew Gorion, didn't you? You must have, considering you and <CHARNAME> grew up together.~
@35   = ~Yeah, I did. I saw how much he cared about <CHARNAME>.~
@36   = ~I don't doubt that. I just envied you a little. I feel as though I could understand you both better if I had known him, or the library you lived in.~
@37   = ~I think you know us well enough, Will. It's not like we can even imagine the life you must have lived. Having a real, whole family and a proper home, nothing like our stone monastery filled with books and boring old monks. With our differences, I think we understand enough about each other, don't ya think?~
@38   = ~Heh, you may be right about that.~
@39   = ~Do you still suffer from nightmares about your husband, Jaheira?~
@40   = ~I have made it clear before that such concerns are mine alone, but if you must know, then... yes. He was my love, after all. I cannot forget the sight of him when... when we...=~
@41   = ~I understand. You don't have to say any more. I only wish I could help you make things easier somehow.~
@42   = ~That is not what I want, Will, and I thought you'd have understood that. These memories of Khalid are all I have left to mourn.~
@43   = ~I'm sorry. I should've made my point clearer. You should remember him, yes, but it's the best parts of your memory of him that you should treasure, not the worst.~
@44   = ~I believe I can recall both as I please. It is only right that I remember all aspects of Khalid's life. I appreciate your concern, but once again, I request that you leave me to settle this alone.~
@45   = ~I'm telling you, Will, I still think that turnips are as effective in repelling vampires as garlic. Perhaps no one has discovered it, but I doubt it's a matter of the ingredients used, but the vegetable's potency! My Jansen family marketed turnips are capable of performing miracles, both in culinary and combat situations!~
@46   = ~I still think the power of garlic in fighting vampires is just a myth, Jan. Otherwise, any spice or general goods merchant could have easily dispatched Bodhi's coven. That would have been ridiculous.~
@47   = ~Hmm, well, that was only a matter of urgency, as you recall. We had that elven lanthorn to deal with, after all, there was no time to take a detour to the local market. Had we the time, I would have convinced <CHARNAME> to have purchased garlic, and my family to offer a wealthy supply of our best turnips for a vampire extermination frenzy!~
@48   = ~Right... Jan, let's just this argument about using vegetables as a weapon against vampires and stick with our swords and magic, with a wooden stake and holy water in our reserves. At least we know those work.~
@49   = ~You know, Keldorn, the title of your god Torm... 'the Loyal Fury', was it? It has always confused me.~
@50   = ~It is straightforward enough once you stop to think. The faithful of Torm are devoted to loyalty above all. As for fury... perhaps it is associated with his fearlessness in battle. Tales of Torm's valor in the Time of Troubles, in which he sacrificed his avatar in a duel against the evil god Bane, as well as his countless battles with Bahamut, must have inspired the moniker.~
@51   = ~Bahamut? There's a god I'm not sure I've ever heard of.~
@52   = ~Bahamut is less well known than other gods of righteous ideals, but perhaps you would know of him as the King of Good Dragons or the Lord of the North Wind. A patron of good dragons who takes on the form of a platinum dragon and one of Torm's greatest allies, often serving as his mount.~
@53   = ~I suppose there are still many gods out there that I know nothing about me. I've never heard of Bahamut, but it'd be quite a sight to see him and Torm fighting side by side. Suddenly the name 'Loyal Fury' doesn't surprise me anymore.~
@54   = ~Oy, mojo-maker! What're ye lookin' at me way fer, eh? Me axe ain't fer ye to touch, not unless yer willing to risk yer fingers, heh heh.~
@55   = ~I wasn't thinking of touching your axe, Korgan.~
@56   = ~Do ye think me to be blind, boy? I've seen ye eyein' it many times! Har, not that I could blame ye. Most would give an arm or a leg fer the chance. No, actually, that be me takin' their arms an' legs with me axe. Heh!~
@57   = ~No, I really wasn't planning on it. I doubt that handle is ever cleaned.~
@58   = ~Cleaned? What nonsense be ye spoutin' now, eh? A dead lad with a fear fer filthy shafts? Har!~
@59   = ~I don't even want to know if we're still talking about axes anymore.~
@60   = ~Do you ever miss your home, Mazzy? We are quite a long way from Trademeet now.~
@61   = ~It would please me greatly to see my home and kin once more, aye, but my duty is of utmost importance to me at the moment, Will.~
@62   = ~Does it help to think that your family is waiting for you to return, safe and sound?~
@63   = ~It brings me comfort, aye. Even if I know I cannot see them again for some time for a good cause, the thought that I am being remembered is more than encouraging. It's rather comforting. It helps me to believe that my current course is right.~
@64   = ~What do you mean by that?~
@65   = ~Perhaps if my cause was unjust, then my family would have tried to keep me from this path. But they did not. I follow <CHARNAME> not only out of my own judgment, but also theirs.~
@66   = ~Minsc, I wonder, does Boo still often think of Dynaheir?~
@67   = ~Of course! Boo thinks of her at every moment, and invokes her name in each battle! A true companion would settle for nothing less.~
@68   = ~I feel as if her spirit knows... knows that both of you, are fighting on in her name. And she would want you to know that.~
@69   = ~Do you hear this, Boo? Our wise shaman is acting as messenger for Dynaheir! She must be proud of us both!~
@70   = ~Thank you, Will, our spiritual friend! Now Minsc and Boo may kick evil butts with even greater pride!~
@71   = ~Yes, Nalia... I believe your father is watching over you. Spirits will observe those they care for the most. They do not forget the things that mattered in their lives.~
@72   = ~It's comforting to know that. I can feel as though my father is at my side in every moment, lending me his courage and strength.~
@73   = ~I feel he shared enough while he still lived. After all, you have grown into a fine and strong woman, Nalia.~
@74   = ~I can't deny that. Father's always supported me, especially when I was feeling down. Whenever I was sick and bedridden, he would be at my bedside whenever I was awake, comforting me any way he could.~
@75   = ~So he cared about you more than anything in life, and that has not changed in death. Such things never change, Nalia, Believe me.~
@76   = ~If you had the power to... would you have destroyed that sphere that you despised so much?~
@77   = ~Your question is a strange one, Will. Once I would have said yes for its magic alone. Now... I must consider. I believe I would, if only because I see no purpose in it but to endanger others. Had I the power, I would shatter it, or sent it to the Nine Hells where no one would see it again.~
@78   = ~Wouldn't that be even worse? What if someone used the sphere to bring fiends to this plane?~
@79   = ~It was a figure of speech, Will. Of course I would rather see it destroyed above all else. It is a dangerous thing, and I cannot see it ever being anything more.~
@80   = ~But what if it could be? What if someone truly found a way to use its power for good?~
@81   = ~Perhaps it is possible, but... it is always easier for such things to fall into the hands of evil. The risks would be far too high.~
@82   = ~I see this... thing is still among us. With your presence, ghost, I am beginning to understand the appeal of undead to practitioners of necromancy. You obey, you do not rest, and need for nothing. You exist to please the one holding your leash.~
@83   = ~You're mistaken in two forms, Viconia. Firstly, I have enough freedom to act in my own choice. Secondly, I would never please one I did not have an attraction towards.~
@84   = ~Good. At least to know that there is no need to restrain yourself, athiyk.~
@85   = ~Sex is for everyone, no? Love is for your chosen one.~
@86   = ~Love? I know little of such a naive concept. March, athiyk. This talk is a waste of time better spent in other ways.~
@87   = ~Hahaha... I see that I am not the only lost soul <CHARNAME> has gathered. This one does not even possess a body! Ha! Have you grown fond towards the dead, <PRO_BROTHERSISTER>?~
@88   = ~I think <CHARNAME> knows how to choose allies carefully. But I was surprised that he let you, someone <PRO_HESHE> killed once of all people, join us. You are unpredictable, even to me, Sarevok.~
@89   = ~Is that so, spirit? Am I then to assume that you bear at least a little fear towards me? Hmph. It is good to see that I still command that much presence.~
@90   = ~I do not fear you, Sarevok. You have proven that you are mortal, just as anyone else. I only consider you interesting.~
@91   = ~Interesting? Do tell, shaman. I have no doubt that your words will amuse me, at least.~
@92   = ~Many spirits whisper of you, of your past, present and future. They wait to see whether you have changed, or whether you will bring doom upon yourself once more.~
@93   = ~You. Healer. Put yourself to good use and prepare some aloe ointment. This blasted armor is chafing my groin.~
@94   = ~I would rather you ask instead of command, Dorn. I am no servant of yours. We are equals here as <CHARNAME>'s companions.~
@95   = ~You have quite the audacity to believe I would consider you worthy enough to be an equal, ghost. I admit you do not surrender easily, but your power is a disappointment.~
@96   = ~What more would you ask of me? Should I personally relieve your chafing with my hands? Perhaps fondle your rocks to please you? I don't particularly care if you consider me a companion or not, Dorn, for the only approval that matters to me is <CHARNAME>'s, not yours.~
@97   = ~Hmph. I doubt one without body nor... stamina would be up for the task of satisfying me regardless. If you will not do as I order, then begone. I will not stand for this waste of time.~
@98   = ~Wait, are you telling me that you turned a merchant... into a puffball?~
@99   = ~Hey, it was an accident! The lecher was trying to charge me for more than the spell scroll was worth! All because I upset him for not complimenting his looks, ugh. Well, that was before I polymorphed him. He looked sorta cute as a puffball.~
@100  = ~It doesn't sound like an accident if that was the case.~
@101  = ~No, well, I guess when you put it like that... um, I can explain...~
@102  = ~Neera...?~
@103  = ~Okay, so I was trying to change his pet cat. You know, to teach him a lesson for messing with me, but something went wrong, I guess. I don't know if I misfired, or the spell just went wrong on its own, but it hit him instead. The cat was delighted, of course. It found its master much more interesting as a ball of wool.~
@104  = ~But you did change him back eventually... right?~
@105  = ~Well... almost. I tried.~
@106  = ~'Almost'? What do you mean you tried?~
@107  = ~Er, I really didn't want to get in trouble, so I casted a counterspell to reverse the spell and... well, he grew his arms and legs back, but his hair had all gone. There were just tiny blue mushooms onh is head. Could've been worse, I guess....~
@108  = ~Huh... sounds like quite a "fungi" situation.~
@109  = ~Rasaad, when we last fought our enemies, I could barely see your fists as they struck. How did you learn to attack so quickly?~
@110  = ~A lot of training and balance, my friend. One must train both body and mind to act with such speed and reflex. These skills are developed from the mental and physical unity I have achieved. But despite that, I have but started on my path towards becoming a true master. There is still much to learn.~
@111  = ~I can hardly imagine how much stronger you'll become, Rasaad. Spirits already talk of your abilities.~
@112  = ~Thank you for assuring me, Will, but I do not train to impress others - neither the living, nor... the dead.~
@113  = ~Did you have any sympathy when Bodhi was destroyed, Hexxat?~
@114  = ~No.~
@115  = ~I thought perhaps you of all people would have understood her.~
@116  = ~I may have, if I had the incentive. But she was our foe, so why would I have any sympathy?~
@117  = ~I never thanked you, Wilson. You saved me in our last battle against the undead, when they had broken my spiritual structure. You broke their bones first.~
@118  = ~Snuffle growl.~
@119  = ~Oh yes, I'm feeling much better. Although I don't feel pain like you do. I just feel... incomplete.~
@120  = ~Snuff?~
@121  = ~No, it's not... pleasant. I hope you never have to feel it yourself~
@122  = ~Rff. Roar.~
@123  = ~Well said, my friend! Well said.~
